# TypeScript

## Source of Truth for Data Types

The Supabase-generated `Database` type in `types/supabase.ts` is the single source of truth for all database shapes. Derive domain types from it — never redefine them manually.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — manually redefined type that can drift from DB
type Spot = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  city_id: string;
};

// ✅ GOOD — derived from generated types
export type Spot = Database['public']['Tables']['spots']['Row'];
export type NewSpot = Database['public']['Tables']['spots']['Insert'];
export type SpotUpdate = Database['public']['Tables']['spots']['Update'];
```

## Function Signatures

Always type function parameters. Always type the return value of non-trivial functions (anything beyond a one-liner arrow). React components are exempt — let inference handle `JSX.Element`.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
const createSpot = async (data) => { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
const createSpot = async (data: NewSpot): Promise<Spot> => { ... }
```

## No `any`

Never use `any`. Alternatives:
- `unknown` + narrow with a type guard when the shape is truly unknown
- The correct Supabase-derived type when working with DB data
- A generic parameter when the type should be flexible

```ts
// ❌ BAD
const handleResponse = (data: any) => data.name;

// ✅ GOOD
const handleResponse = (data: Spot) => data.name;
```

## No Non-Null Assertions

Avoid the `!` operator. Handle the null/undefined case explicitly.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
const name = user!.name;

// ✅ GOOD
if (!user) return null;
const name = user.name;
```

## No Type Assertions Unless Necessary

Avoid `as SomeType` unless working with a third-party library that returns untyped data. Never use `as any` under any circumstances.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
const spot = data as Spot;

// ✅ GOOD — trust the typed Supabase query return
const { data: spot, error } = await supabase
  .from('spots')
  .select('*')
  .eq('id', id)
  .single();
```

## Union Types Over Enums

Prefer string union types. They are simpler, tree-shakeable, and work naturally with Supabase string columns.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
enum Plan { Free = 'free', Pro = 'pro' }

// ✅ GOOD
type Plan = 'free' | 'pro';
```

## Optional vs Required

Be deliberate — don't make everything optional as a shortcut. A prop or field is optional only if the feature genuinely works without it.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — lazy optionals
type SpotCardProps = {
  spot?: Spot;
  onPress?: () => void;
};

// ✅ GOOD
type SpotCardProps = {
  spot: Spot;
  onPress: (spotId: string) => void;
  showTags?: boolean; // genuinely optional — has a sensible default
};
```

## Generics for Reusable Utilities

Use generics when a utility works across multiple types rather than duplicating logic.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
const groupById = <T extends { id: string }>(items: T[]): Record<string, T> =>
  Object.fromEntries(items.map(item => [item.id, item]));
```
